<Hook>

You reach the river with no pair. The table below says high-card hands are less common than one-pair and two-pair hands among seven-card sets. Should high card suddenly beat them?

</Hook>

<Idea>

### Keep the ranking rule fixed

No. Hold’em uses a fixed ordering of five-card hands. You select the strongest five cards available and compare those. The number of seven-card sets producing a category does not rewrite the rule at showdown. Changing the size of the collection you search changes the frequencies, not the comparison rule.

Look first at five cards. High card is the largest category because many combinations contain five different ranks without forming a straight or flush. A pair requires a rank to appear twice. Two pair requires two such rank matches, while leaving the fifth rank different. The formulas from the previous lesson count these restrictions explicitly.

### What extra cards change

Now imagine adding two more cards. Some five-card high-card hands gain a pair; some gain two pair, a straight, or a flush. A five-card pair can also improve. None of these observations lets us obtain exact seven-card counts by adding “upgrade rates” without considering overlaps. The final seven-card sets must still be counted once by their strongest category.

<FrequencyCounts selected={[0, 1, 2]} />

Expand the three rows. The high-card seven-card count is smaller than both the one-pair and two-pair counts. Its event is restrictive: seven cards must contain no repeated rank, no straight anywhere in the rank set, and no five cards of one suit. Having more cards gives more chances to violate at least one of those restrictions.

### Compare an actual showdown

A concrete showdown makes the distinction clear. Put Ac Kd 8s 6h 3c on the board. A player holding Qd Js has only high card. A player holding 2h 2d has a pair of twos. The pair wins even though that particular way of ending with high card belongs to a less frequent seven-card category. The board is already known, so neither unconditional category frequency is the relevant chance of winning this hand.

Also distinguish category frequency from a particular hand’s strength within its category. An ace-high flush beats a king-high flush, but both contribute to the same flush counter. Suits do not break ties. If both players' best five have equal ranks, the pot splits even when their unused cards differ.

Select high card, one pair, and two pair in the experiment. Compare their exact reference lines, then run each with the same sample count. The experiment measures how often a category occurs when dealing seven fresh cards from the full deck. It does not deal a specific board and opponent range; the Lab handles that different conditional question.

### Ask what the table is sampling

The frequency table begins before any particular hand or board is known. It asks about a uniformly dealt set from a full deck. At a real showdown, many cards are already fixed, and you are comparing specific best fives. These are different experiments even if both use the word “hand.”

A useful habit is to state the denominator in words. “All seven-card sets from a full deck” belongs to a category frequency. “All legal remaining runouts against these opposing cards” belongs to a conditional matchup. Neither answer can replace the other simply because it is printed as a percentage.

<Shortcut />

